insert into d (name, yr, txt) values
  ('Fall of Constantinople', 1453, 'On 29 May 1453 Sultan Mehmed II''s army stormed Constantinople after a 53-day siege, and the last Byzantine emperor, Constantine XI, died in the final assault. The fall ended the eastern Roman Empire after more than 1,100 years and gave the Ottomans a capital that straddled Europe and Asia. Its giant siege cannons showed that gunpowder had made old city walls obsolete, and historians often use 1453 as a marker for the end of the Middle Ages.'),
  ('Ottoman conquest of Constantinople', 1453, 'Mehmed II, only 21 years old, captured Constantinople on 29 May 1453 after a siege of almost two months, using enormous bombards and ships hauled overland into the Golden Horn. The conquest ended the Byzantine Empire, the last remnant of ancient Rome, and made the city the Ottoman capital. It is traditionally treated as the moment the medieval world gave way to the early modern one.'),
  ('Siege of Belgrade (1456)', 1456, 'In July 1456 Mehmed II besieged Belgrade, then called Nándorfehérvár, to open the road into central Europe. A relief force under John Hunyadi and the friar John of Capistrano broke the Ottoman blockade on the Danube and then beat off the assault on the city. Hunyadi died of plague a few weeks later, but the victory stopped Ottoman advance up the Danube for about 65 years, and the noon church bell that Pope Callixtus III had ordered for the crusade became remembered as its commemoration.'),
  ('Battle of Towton', 1461, 'On Palm Sunday 1461, in a snowstorm near Towton in Yorkshire, the Yorkists under Edward of March crushed the Lancastrian army of Henry VI in a fight that lasted about ten hours. It is often called the largest and bloodiest battle ever fought on English soil, with a very high share of the men involved killed, many in the rout. The victory put Edward on the throne as Edward IV, although the Wars of the Roses were far from over.'),
  ('Battle of Vajkal', 1465, 'In April 1465 Skanderbeg''s Albanians defeated an Ottoman army led by Ballaban Pasha, an Albanian-born Ottoman commander, in the Vajkal valley near Dibra. Both sides suffered heavy losses, and several of Skanderbeg''s officers were captured and executed. It is remembered as one of the hard-fought victories of his resistance against the Ottomans, which lasted from 1443 until his death in 1468 and made him a European hero and an Albanian national symbol.'),
  ('Battle of Vaslui', 1475, 'On 10 January 1475 Stephen the Great of Moldavia ambushed a much larger Ottoman army under Hadım Süleyman Pasha in a marshy river valley near Vaslui, in midwinter. The Ottomans suffered a crushing defeat, though the figures given by contemporaries are certainly exaggerated. The victory made Stephen famous across Christian Europe, and Pope Sixtus IV praised him as the ''athleta Christi'' (champion of Christ), even though little practical help followed.'),
  ('Battle of Breadfield', 1479, 'On 13 October 1479 a Hungarian-Transylvanian army led by Pál Kinizsi and István Báthory, joined by Serbian and Wallachian allies, destroyed a large Ottoman raiding force on the Breadfield (Kenyérmező) in Transylvania. Kinizsi, famed for his huge strength, led the decisive heavy-cavalry charge. It was the largest battle fought in Transylvania up to then, and the Ottomans did not raid Transylvania and southern Hungary on such a scale again for many years.'),
  ('Great Stand on the Ugra River', 1480, 'In autumn 1480 the armies of Ivan III of Moscow and Khan Akhmat of the Great Horde faced each other across the Ugra River for weeks without a real battle. Akhmat''s expected Lithuanian reinforcements never arrived, and in November he withdrew. Russian tradition treats this standoff as the end of the ''Tatar yoke'', because Moscow never paid tribute to the Horde again.'),
  ('Battle of Bosworth Field', 1485, 'On 22 August 1485 Henry Tudor''s smaller army defeated King Richard III near Market Bosworth in Leicestershire after Lord Stanley''s men joined the fight against the king. Richard was killed, the last English king to die in battle. Henry became Henry VII and founded the Tudor dynasty, and Bosworth is usually taken as the end of the Wars of the Roses.'),
  ('Columbus reaches the Americas', 1492, 'Sailing for Spain''s Catholic Monarchs in search of a western route to Asia, Christopher Columbus made landfall in the Bahamas on 12 October 1492. His four voyages brought the Americas into permanent contact with Europe and set off the Columbian Exchange of crops, animals and diseases. They also began centuries of colonization that devastated Indigenous populations.'),
  ('European sighting of the Caribbean', 1492, 'In 1492 Columbus''s three ships crossed the Atlantic and reached the Bahamas, then Cuba and Hispaniola, the first lasting European contact with the Caribbean. The Santa María was wrecked off Hispaniola and its timbers were used to build the small fort of La Navidad. News of the voyage triggered rapid Spanish settlement and an exchange of plants, animals and diseases that reshaped both hemispheres.'),
  ('Fall of Granada', 1492, 'On 2 January 1492 the last Nasrid ruler, Boabdil, surrendered Granada to Ferdinand and Isabella, ending a ten-year war. It was the last Muslim-ruled state on the Iberian Peninsula and completed the centuries-long Christian Reconquista. The same year Spain expelled its unconverted Jews and backed Columbus''s voyage, which makes 1492 a turning point in Spanish and world history.'),
  ('Battle of Fornovo', 1495, 'Retreating from Naples, Charles VIII of France fought his way through a larger Italian League army at Fornovo, near Parma, on 6 July 1495. The French held the field and escaped over the Alps, but the campaign achieved nothing, because all their conquests in Italy were lost. It was the first big pitched battle of the Italian Wars, a series of conflicts that would draw in much of Europe for more than sixty years.'),
  ('Safavid Empire founded', 1501, 'In 1501 the teenage Ismail I took Tabriz and proclaimed himself shah, founding the Safavid dynasty. He made Twelver Shi''a Islam the state religion, a decision that reshaped the religious identity of Iran for centuries and set it apart from its Sunni Ottoman and Uzbek neighbours. Safavid rule lasted until 1736, and it is often seen as the start of modern Iranian history.'),
  ('Battle of Garigliano', 1503, 'On 29 December 1503 a Spanish army under Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba, the ''Great Captain'', routed the French at the Garigliano River north of Naples after a bold winter crossing. The victory secured the Kingdom of Naples for Ferdinand of Aragon and ended French ambitions in southern Italy. It also showed the strength of Gonzalo''s new mixed formations of pikemen and arquebusiers, forerunners of the famous Spanish tercios.'),
  ('Battle of Ravenna', 1512, 'On Easter Sunday, 11 April 1512, French and Ferrarese troops under Gaston de Foix beat the Spanish-Papal army of the Holy League outside Ravenna in one of the bloodiest battles of the Italian Wars. Artillery was used on a scale not seen before, and the Spanish guns inflicted terrible losses before the French cavalry broke the enemy line. Gaston, the brilliant 22-year-old French commander, was killed in the pursuit, and the loss of his leadership turned the victory into a strategic setback for France.'),
  ('Battle of Flodden', 1513, 'On 9 September 1513 an English army under the Earl of Surrey destroyed the invading Scottish army of James IV at Flodden, near Branxton in Northumberland. James was killed along with much of the Scottish nobility, making it the worst military disaster in Scottish history, and he was the last British monarch to die in battle. It was the largest battle ever fought between the two kingdoms and left Scotland with an infant king, James V, and years of instability.'),
  ('Battle of Novara', 1513, 'On 6 June 1513 Swiss pikemen defending Milan for Duke Maximilian Sforza surprised and routed a French army near Novara. The defeat forced France to withdraw from Italy altogether. The Swiss again showed that their dense pike columns could beat armies built around cavalry and artillery, although two years later at Marignano the French guns would answer them.'),
  ('Battle of Chaldiran', 1514, 'On 23 August 1514 the Ottoman army of Selim I defeated the Safavid forces of Shah Ismail I at Chaldiran in eastern Anatolia. Ottoman muskets and cannons proved decisive against Safavid cavalry. The victory secured eastern Anatolia and Upper Mesopotamia for the Ottomans, halted Safavid expansion to the west and shook Ismail''s reputation for invincibility among his Qizilbash followers; the rivalry between the two empires lasted for generations.'),
  ('Battle of Orsha', 1514, 'On 8 September 1514 a smaller Lithuanian-Polish army under Hetman Konstanty Ostrogski defeated a large Muscovite army near Orsha on the Dnieper. Ostrogski''s cavalry feigned a retreat to draw the Muscovites forward, and a counterattack by his reserve cavalry, backed by artillery, then broke them. The victory did not win back Smolensk, which Moscow had taken weeks earlier, but it stopped the Muscovite advance and remains a celebrated national victory in Belarus and Lithuania.'),
  ('Battle of Marignano', 1515, 'On 13-14 September 1515 the newly crowned French king Francis I defeated the Swiss Confederation at Marignano near Milan after two days of fierce fighting. French artillery and cavalry broke the Swiss pike columns that had dominated European battlefields for decades. The Perpetual Peace of 1516 that followed ended Swiss expansion in Italy and began a long alliance between France and the Swiss, who supplied mercenaries to French kings.'),
  ('Ottoman conquest of Egypt', 1517, 'In 1516-1517 Sultan Selim I destroyed the Mamluk Sultanate, defeating it at Marj Dabiq in Syria and then at Ridaniya outside Cairo. The Ottomans took Syria, Egypt and the holy cities of Mecca and Medina, enormously enlarging their empire. The sultan became protector of Islam''s holiest sites, and the empire''s centre of gravity shifted from the Balkans and Anatolia toward the Arab world.'),
  ('Protestant Reformation begins', 1517, 'On 31 October 1517 the monk Martin Luther published his Ninety-five Theses in Wittenberg, attacking the sale of indulgences by the Church. Spread rapidly by the printing press, his ideas split Western Christianity into Catholic and Protestant churches. The religious conflicts that followed shaped European politics, education and warfare for the next two centuries.'),
  ('Diet of Worms', 1521, 'In April 1521 Martin Luther stood before Emperor Charles V and the imperial Diet at Worms and refused to take back his writings. Charles then issued the Edict of Worms, declaring Luther an outlaw whose books were to be burned. Luther was hidden in Wartburg Castle by his protector Frederick of Saxony, and the confrontation hardened the split that became the Protestant Reformation.'),
  ('Fall of Tenochtitlan', 1521, 'On 13 August 1521, after a siege of about eleven weeks, Hernán Cortés and thousands of indigenous allies captured Tenochtitlan, the island capital of the Aztecs. The city had been weakened by smallpox, and Tlaxcalans and other enemies of the Aztecs supplied much of the fighting force. Its fall ended Aztec rule, and the Spanish built Mexico City on its ruins as the centre of New Spain.'),
  ('Battle of Bicocca', 1522, 'On 27 April 1522 an Imperial-Spanish army under Prospero Colonna, dug in behind a sunken road and ditches, shattered a French-Swiss army under Lautrec near Milan. The Swiss pikemen who attacked the position were cut down by arquebus fire and lost heavily. Bicocca showed that firearms behind field fortifications could defeat massed pike assaults, and France lost Lombardy.'),
  ('Battle of Frankenhausen', 1525, 'On 14-15 May 1525 the armies of Hesse and Saxony, led by Philip of Hesse and Duke George, crushed a poorly armed peasant host at Frankenhausen in Thuringia. The preacher Thomas Müntzer, the rebels'' spiritual leader, was captured and later executed. The battle effectively ended the German Peasants'' War, the largest popular uprising in Europe before the French Revolution.'),
  ('Battle of Pavia', 1525, 'On 24 February 1525 the Habsburg army of Charles V crushed the French besiegers of Pavia and captured King Francis I himself. Spanish arquebusiers played a decisive part against the French heavy cavalry, and much of the French nobility was killed or taken. Francis was held prisoner in Madrid and forced to sign a humiliating treaty, and Habsburg power became dominant in Italy.'),
  ('Battle of Mohacs', 1526, 'On 29 August 1526 Sultan Suleiman the Magnificent destroyed the Hungarian army of King Louis II at Mohács in about two hours. Louis drowned while fleeing, and a large part of the Hungarian nobility and clergy died with him. The defeat ended medieval Hungary as an independent power, and the country was split between the Habsburgs, the Ottomans and Transylvania for the next 150 years.'),
  ('Sack of Rome', 1527, 'On 6 May 1527 the unpaid and mutinous troops of Emperor Charles V stormed Rome and spent days looting the city, killing many and holding others for ransom. Pope Clement VII fled to the Castel Sant''Angelo and later had to make peace on the Emperor''s terms. The shock is often seen as the end of the High Renaissance in Rome and showed that even the papacy was at the mercy of Habsburg power.'),
  ('First Ottoman Siege of Vienna', 1529, 'Sultan Suleiman the Magnificent besieged Vienna from 27 September to 15 October 1529 with a vast army, while only about 20,000 defenders under Niklas Graf Salm held the city. Early snow, heavy rain and supply problems forced the Ottomans to retreat. It was the furthest west Suleiman''s armies ever reached, and it showed that the Habsburgs could hold the line on the Danube until the second siege of 1683.'),
  ('Battle of Obertyn', 1531, 'On 22 August 1531 a Polish army under Hetman Jan Tarnowski defeated the Moldavian army of Voivode Petru Rareș near Obertyn, south of the Dniester. Moldavia lost thousands of men and all its cannons, while Polish losses were only a few hundred. The victory won back the disputed region of Pokuttya for Poland and is remembered as one of the great successes of Polish arms in the 16th century.'),
  ('Battle of Cajamarca', 1532, 'On 16 November 1532 Francisco Pizarro and fewer than 200 Spaniards ambushed the Inca emperor Atahualpa in the main plaza of Cajamarca, killing thousands of his unarmed attendants and capturing him. His large army outside the town fled instead of fighting. The seizure of the Sapa Inca opened the Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire, and Atahualpa was executed the following year even after paying an enormous ransom in gold and silver.'),
  ('Battle of Quipaipán', 1532, 'At Quipaipán, near Cusco, in about April 1532 the generals of Atahualpa defeated the army of his half-brother Huáscar, who was captured. It was the decisive battle of the Inca Civil War, which had split the empire after the death of Huayna Capac. The war left the Inca state exhausted and divided just before Pizarro arrived, which helped a handful of Spaniards conquer it.'),
  ('Spanish conquest of Peru', 1532, 'In 1532 Francisco Pizarro and about 168 Spanish soldiers captured the Inca emperor Atahualpa at Cajamarca and, exploiting an empire already weakened by civil war and smallpox, went on to take Cusco in 1533. The Spanish used steel weapons, horses, firearms and large numbers of native allies against the Inca. The conquest gave Spain the silver of Potosí and made Peru the centre of its South American empire for centuries.'),
  ('Battle of Las Salinas', 1538, 'On 26 April 1538 the forces of Hernando and Gonzalo Pizarro defeated those of Diego de Almagro at Las Salinas, near Cusco, in a civil war between Spain''s conquistadors over who controlled the city. Almagro was captured and executed soon afterwards. The fight showed that the conquerors would turn on each other over the spoils of the Inca Empire, and it began years of civil wars in Peru.'),
  ('Battle of Ceresole', 1544, 'On 14 April 1544 a French army under the Count of Enghien defeated the Imperial-Spanish forces of the Marquis del Vasto at Ceresole d''Alba in Piedmont. French cavalry and Swiss pikemen won a hard-fought battle, but the French failed to exploit it and no territory changed hands. Ceresole is remembered as a rare pitched-battle victory for France in the Italian Wars and as a bloody example of how costly the open battles of the period were.'),
  ('Council of Trent', 1545, 'The Council of Trent met in the northern Italian city of Trento in three periods between 1545 and 1563 to answer the Protestant Reformation. It defined Catholic teaching on scripture, salvation and the sacraments, ordered the creation of seminaries to train priests, and condemned abuses such as the sale of church offices. Its decrees shaped the Catholic Church for roughly four centuries, until the Second Vatican Council.'),
  ('Battle of Pinkie Cleugh', 1547, 'On 10 September 1547 an English army under the Duke of Somerset routed a larger Scottish army near Musselburgh, east of Edinburgh, helped by ships'' guns firing from the Firth of Forth. It is remembered in Scotland as ''Black Saturday'' and was the last pitched battle between the two kingdoms before the Union of the Crowns. The victory failed in its aim, because the young Mary, Queen of Scots, was sent to France to marry the French heir instead of Edward VI of England.'),
  ('Peace of Augsburg', 1555, 'On 25 September 1555 the Peace of Augsburg ended open religious war inside the Holy Roman Empire. Under the principle later summed up as ''cuius regio, eius religio'', each prince could choose Lutheranism or Catholicism for his own territory, and subjects had to follow or emigrate. It brought about 60 years of relative calm in Germany but left Calvinists excluded, a flaw that helped lead to the Thirty Years'' War.'),
  ('Battle of Dreux', 1562, 'On 19 December 1562 Catholic forces under Anne de Montmorency defeated the Huguenots led by the Prince of Condé at Dreux, the first major battle of the French Wars of Religion. Commanders on both sides were captured, which was unusual even for the time. The Catholic victory did not end the conflict, which flared again and again for more than thirty years, but it showed how bitter the religious civil war would be.'),
  ('Founding of St. Augustine', 1565, 'On 8 September 1565 the Spanish admiral Pedro Menéndez de Avilés founded St. Augustine on the Florida coast, after being sent to remove a French Huguenot colony nearby. It is the oldest continuously inhabited European-founded settlement in the contiguous United States, 42 years older than Jamestown and 55 years older than Plymouth. It anchored Spain''s claim to Florida and protected the sea route used by its treasure fleets.'),
  ('Great Siege of Malta', 1565, 'From 18 May to 8 September 1565 an Ottoman force many times larger than the defenders besieged the Knights Hospitaller on Malta. Grand Master Jean de Valette''s Knights and Maltese held out through the bloody fall of Fort St Elmo and repeated assaults on the other forts, until a Spanish relief force landed and the Ottomans withdrew. The victory ended Ottoman hopes of taking the central Mediterranean and made Valette, after whom Malta''s capital Valletta is named, a hero of Christian Europe.'),
  ('Siege of Szigetvár', 1566, 'In August and September 1566 Nikola Zrinski''s garrison of about 2,300 men held the small Hungarian fortress of Szigetvár against Suleiman the Magnificent''s huge army for a month. When the fort was lost, Zrinski led his last men out in a charge and died. Suleiman himself died in his tent during the siege, and the delay meant the Ottoman army never reached Vienna that year; Zrinski became a hero for both Croats and Hungarians.'),
  ('Battle of Heiligerlee', 1568, 'On 23 May 1568 rebels led by Louis of Nassau defeated a Spanish force at Heiligerlee in Groningen, the first Dutch victory of the Eighty Years'' War. His brother Adolf of Nassau was killed in the fighting, and their elder brother William of Orange would lead the revolt. The success was short-lived, as the Duke of Alba crushed the rebels two months later, but it is remembered as the beginning of the war that created the Dutch Republic.'),
  ('Battle of Moncontour', 1569, 'On 3 October 1569 the royal Catholic army under Henry, Duke of Anjou, later Henry III, defeated the Huguenots under Admiral Coligny at Moncontour in Poitou. The Huguenots lost perhaps half of their infantry, though most of their cavalry escaped. The victory weakened the Protestant cause for a time, but Coligny rebuilt his army and the peace of 1570 gave the Huguenots limited rights.'),
  ('Battle of Lepanto', 1571, 'On 7 October 1571 a Holy League fleet from Spain, Venice and the papacy, commanded by Don John of Austria, destroyed most of the Ottoman fleet in the Gulf of Patras. It was one of the largest naval battles in history and the last major clash of oared galleys. The victory ended the idea that the Ottomans were unbeatable at sea, although they rebuilt their fleet within a year; Miguel de Cervantes, the future author of Don Quixote, was wounded in the fight.'),
  ('St. Bartholomew''s Day massacre', 1572, 'In the days after 24 August 1572, Catholic mobs and royal troops in Paris and then other French cities murdered thousands of Huguenots, just days after the wedding of the Protestant Henry of Navarre to the king''s sister. The killing began with an attack on Huguenot leaders in Paris, including Admiral Coligny, and spread through the country. The massacre shocked Protestant Europe, deepened the French Wars of Religion and became a lasting symbol of religious violence.'),
  ('Battle of Ksar el Kebir', 1578, 'On 4 August 1578, in northern Morocco, a Moroccan army defeated the Portuguese crusade of the young King Sebastian in the ''Battle of the Three Kings'', named for the three rulers who died: Sebastian, the deposed sultan al-Mutawakkil and Sultan Abd al-Malik. Much of the Portuguese nobility was killed or captured. With no adult heir, Portugal fell to Spain''s Philip II in 1580, so the battle led to sixty years without Portuguese independence.'),
  ('Battle of Coutras', 1587, 'On 20 October 1587 Henry of Navarre, the Protestant leader and future Henry IV, defeated a royal army under the Duke of Joyeuse at Coutras, and Joyeuse was killed while trying to surrender. It was the Huguenots'' first great victory in the field, won by disciplined gunfire and cavalry tactics. It strengthened Henry''s claim to be the next king of France.'),
  ('Spanish Armada', 1588, 'In 1588 Philip II sent the ''Invincible Armada'' of about 130 ships to carry the Spanish army from Flanders across the Channel to invade England. English fireships and gunfire broke the fleet''s formation off Gravelines, and storms wrecked many ships as the survivors sailed home around Scotland and Ireland. The failure saved Elizabeth I''s Protestant England and boosted English naval confidence, though the war with Spain dragged on until 1604.'),
  ('Battle of Ivry', 1590, 'On 14 March 1590 Henry IV of France defeated the Catholic League army of the Duke of Mayenne and its Spanish allies on the plain of Ivry in Normandy. Henry famously told his men to follow his white plume wherever they saw it. The victory opened the way to a siege of Paris, though the city held out, and Henry only entered it in 1594 after converting to Catholicism.'),
  ('Moroccan conquest of Songhai', 1591, 'In 1591 a small Moroccan army equipped with firearms, led by Judar Pasha, crossed the Sahara and routed the much larger Songhai army at Tondibi. The invaders went on to seize the Songhai cities of Gao and Timbuktu. The conquest broke up one of the largest empires in African history and shifted West Africa''s trade and power away from the Niger.'),
  ('Battle of Sisak', 1593, 'On 22 June 1593 an Austro-Croatian relief army, together with the garrison of Sisak, surprised and routed the Bosnian Ottoman army besieging the fortress at the junction of the Sava and Kupa rivers. The Ottoman commander Hasan Pasha was killed and many of his men drowned fleeing across the Kupa. The defeat prompted the Ottomans to declare war on the Habsburgs, beginning the Long Turkish War of 1593-1606.'),
  ('Battle of Mezőkeresztes', 1596, 'On 24-26 October 1596 a Habsburg-Transylvanian army seemed close to beating the Ottomans at Mezőkeresztes in northern Hungary, until its troops began plundering the Ottoman camp. The Ottomans counterattacked and turned the battle, and Sultan Mehmed III, who had wanted to flee, was reportedly persuaded by his tutor to stay. The Ottoman losses were so heavy that they could not exploit the victory, and the Long Turkish War dragged on without a clear winner until 1606.'),
  ('Edict of Nantes', 1598, 'In April 1598 Henry IV of France, himself a former Huguenot who had converted to Catholicism, issued the Edict of Nantes. It kept Catholicism as the state religion but gave Protestant Huguenots freedom of conscience, the right to worship in many places and some fortified towns for their safety. It ended the French Wars of Religion and was a landmark of religious toleration until Louis XIV revoked it in 1685.'),
  ('Battle of Nieuwpoort', 1600, 'On 2 July 1600 Maurice of Nassau''s Dutch army defeated a Spanish army in the dunes near Nieuwpoort on the Flemish coast, after an unplanned encounter that nearly went badly for the Dutch. It was one of Maurice''s greatest victories and showed that his drill and tactical reforms could beat Spain''s veterans. He still failed to take the port of Dunkirk as planned, and the war went on until the Twelve Years'' Truce of 1609.'),
  ('Battle of Kircholm', 1605, 'On 27 September 1605 a much smaller Polish-Lithuanian army under Jan Karol Chodkiewicz destroyed a Swedish army besieging Riga at Kircholm, in modern Latvia. The famous winged hussars charged repeatedly and broke the Swedes, who lost thousands while Polish-Lithuanian losses were tiny. Kircholm is considered the high point of the hussars and one of the most one-sided victories of the age, although the war with Sweden went on for decades.'),
  ('Jamestown founded', 1607, 'In May 1607 about 104 English colonists founded Jamestown on the James River, the first permanent English settlement in North America. Disease, hunger and conflict with the Powhatan people killed most settlers in the early years, and the colony survived only through hard leadership and later tobacco farming. Jamestown was the capital of Virginia until 1699, and the first enslaved Africans in English North America arrived nearby in 1619.'),
  ('Thirty Years'' War', 1618, 'The war began in 1618 when Protestant Bohemian nobles threw royal officials out of a window in Prague and challenged Habsburg rule, and it grew into a struggle that drew in most of Europe. Fought mainly in Germany, it killed millions of soldiers and civilians through battle, famine and disease, and some regions lost more than half their population. It ended with the Peace of Westphalia in 1648, which reshaped the map of Europe and is often seen as the birth of the modern system of sovereign states.'),
  ('Battle of White Mountain', 1620, 'On 8 November 1620 the Catholic army of Emperor Ferdinand II, under Bucquoy and Tilly, crushed the Bohemian Protestant army of Frederick V outside Prague in about an hour. Frederick, the ''Winter King'', fled, and Bohemia lost its independence. Habsburg rule was imposed, Protestant leaders were executed or exiled, and the Czech lands stayed under Catholic Habsburg control for three centuries.'),
  ('Mayflower Compact', 1620, 'In November 1620, as the Mayflower lay off Cape Cod, the male passengers, Separatist ''Pilgrims'' and other settlers, signed an agreement to form a body politic and obey its laws. It was written because they had landed outside the territory of their charter, and it became the first governing document of Plymouth Colony. It is often cited as an early example of self-government by the consent of the governed.'),
  ('Battle of Wimpfen', 1622, 'On 6 May 1622 the Catholic League army of Count Tilly, joined by Spanish troops, defeated the Protestant army of Margrave George Frederick of Baden-Durlach at Wimpfen in southwest Germany. It was an early battle of the Palatinate phase of the Thirty Years'' War. The victory left the Palatinate open to Catholic occupation and showed that the Protestant cause could not rely on small mercenary armies.'),
  ('Battle of Lutter', 1626, 'On 27 August 1626 Count Tilly''s Catholic League army defeated King Christian IV of Denmark at Lutter in Lower Saxony, after the Danes'' attack was shattered by artillery fire. Christian fled the field, and thousands of his men were killed or captured. The defeat ended Denmark''s serious attempt to fight in Germany, and the Danes left the war in 1629.'),
  ('Battle of Breitenfeld', 1631, 'On 17 September 1631 the Swedish army of Gustavus Adolphus and its Saxon allies defeated the Imperial-League army of Count Tilly at Breitenfeld, near Leipzig. Tilly, who had won every major battle until then, saw his deep, rigid formations torn apart by the Swedes'' flexible lines and fast-firing field guns. It was the first great Protestant victory of the Thirty Years'' War and opened Germany to the Swedish army.'),
  ('Sack of Magdeburg', 1631, 'On 20-24 May 1631 Imperial and Catholic League troops under Tilly and Pappenheim stormed the Protestant city of Magdeburg, and a great fire destroyed most of it. Around 20,000 people died, defenders and civilians alike, and it is considered the worst massacre of the Thirty Years'' War. The horror was used in Protestant propaganda across Europe, and the city''s name became a byword for destruction.'),
  ('Battle of Lützen', 1632, 'On 16 November 1632 the Swedish army of Gustavus Adolphus narrowly defeated Wallenstein''s Imperial army at Lützen in Saxony, but the king was killed in a cavalry charge during the fog and smoke. Both sides lost heavily, and the Catholic general Pappenheim also died. The Swedes held the field, yet the loss of their charismatic king left the Protestant war effort without its best commander.'),
  ('Battle of Rain', 1632, 'In April 1632 Gustavus Adolphus forced a crossing of the Lech River at Rain in Bavaria under the cover of a heavy artillery barrage. The Catholic League commander, Count Tilly, was mortally wounded and died soon afterwards. The victory opened Bavaria to the Swedes, who occupied Munich the following month.'),
  ('Battle of the Alte Veste', 1632, 'On 3 September 1632 Gustavus Adolphus attacked Wallenstein''s strongly entrenched camp on the Alte Veste hill near Nuremberg, and the Swedish assaults were beaten back with heavy losses. It was one of the few defeats suffered by the Swedish king. The standoff left both armies short of food and led to the larger clash at Lützen two months later.'),
  ('Battle of Nördlingen', 1634, 'On 5-6 September 1634 an Imperial-Spanish army under Ferdinand of Hungary and the Cardinal-Infante crushed the Swedish army and its German allies at Nördlingen in Bavaria, and the Swedish commander Gustav Horn was captured. It was a disaster for Sweden that ended its dominance in southern Germany. Many Protestant princes made peace with the Emperor in 1635, and France then entered the Thirty Years'' War openly.'),
  ('Battle of Wittstock', 1636, 'On 4 October 1636 a Swedish army under Johan Banér and Alexander Leslie defeated a larger Imperial-Saxon army at Wittstock in northern Germany. The victory restored Sweden''s position in Germany after the defeat at Nördlingen and the Peace of Prague had left it nearly isolated. It showed that the Swedish war effort could carry on without its dead king, Gustavus Adolphus.'),
  ('Qing dynasty founded', 1636, 'In 1636 Hong Taiji, ruler of the Manchus, proclaimed the Great Qing dynasty in Mukden, renaming the earlier Later Jin state and taking the title of emperor after the Mongols recognized his rule. He built an army and administration that mixed Manchu, Mongol and Chinese elements. In 1644 the Qing took advantage of China''s collapse to seize Beijing, and the dynasty ruled China until 1912.'),
  ('Battle of Rocroi', 1643, 'On 19 May 1643 the 21-year-old Duke of Enghien, later known as the Great Condé, defeated a Spanish army at Rocroi on the French border, five days after the death of Louis XIII. The Spanish tercios, the pike-and-shot infantry that had dominated European battlefields for about 120 years, fought to the end but were broken. The victory ended the myth of Spanish invincibility and marked the rise of French military power under the young Louis XIV.'),
  ('Battle of Shanhai Pass', 1644, 'On 27 May 1644 the Manchu prince-regent Dorgon, allied with the Ming general Wu Sangui, defeated the rebel leader Li Zicheng at Shanhai Pass, where the Great Wall meets the sea. Li had just taken Beijing and brought the Ming dynasty to an end, and Wu chose to ally with the Manchus rather than submit to him. The Qing army then entered Beijing and went on to conquer China, founding the dynasty that ruled until 1912.'),
  ('Battle of Jankau', 1645, 'On 6 March 1645 the Swedish army under Lennart Torstensson destroyed an Imperial army under Melchior von Hatzfeldt at Jankau in Bohemia, capturing thousands of prisoners and nearly all its artillery. The victory opened the way for a Swedish advance toward Vienna, which threatened the Emperor directly. However, supply problems after decades of devastation stopped the Swedes from exploiting it fully.'),
  ('Peace of Westphalia', 1648, 'In October 1648 the treaties of Osnabrück and Münster ended the Thirty Years'' War, which had killed an estimated 4.5 to 8 million people. They recognized the independence of the Dutch Republic and Switzerland, allowed Calvinism alongside Lutheranism and Catholicism, and gave the princes of the Holy Roman Empire greater independence. The settlement is often cited as the birth of the modern system of sovereign states.'),
  ('Cape Colony established', 1652, 'In April 1652 Jan van Riebeeck landed at Table Bay with a Dutch East India Company expedition to set up a supply station for ships sailing between Europe and Asia. The small outpost grew into a permanent colony as settlers and enslaved people were brought in, and it displaced the Khoikhoi who lived there. It was the beginning of European settlement in southern Africa, which shaped the region''s history through the Dutch and British periods.'),
  ('Battle of Saint Gotthard', 1664, 'On 1 August 1664 an Imperial army under Raimondo Montecuccoli, with French and German allies, defeated the Ottoman army of Grand Vizier Fazıl Ahmed Köprülü on the Raab River at St Gotthard in western Hungary. It was the first significant Christian victory over a large Ottoman army in the open field in years. Yet the Peace of Vasvár that followed was surprisingly generous to the Ottomans, which angered Hungarian nobles and fuelled conspiracies against the Habsburgs.'),
  ('Pueblo Revolt', 1680, 'In August 1680 the Pueblo peoples of what is now New Mexico, led by the religious leader Popé, rose together against Spanish rule, killing hundreds of colonists and most of the Franciscan friars and driving the Spanish out of the province. It was one of the most successful Indigenous uprisings against European colonizers in North America. The Spanish did not return until 1692, and by then they allowed more tolerance for Pueblo religion and culture.'),
  ('Battle of Vienna', 1683, 'On 12 September 1683 a relief army of Habsburg, German and Polish-Lithuanian troops under King John III Sobieski attacked the Ottoman army besieging Vienna. Sobieski''s winged hussars led one of the largest cavalry charges in history from the Kahlenberg heights and broke the Ottoman camp. The victory saved the Habsburg capital and was the turning point of the Great Turkish War, in which the Ottomans were driven out of most of Hungary.'),
  ('Siege of Buda (1686)', 1686, 'In September 1686 the Holy League army under Charles of Lorraine stormed Buda after a siege of almost three months, ending nearly 150 years of Ottoman rule over the old Hungarian capital. Volunteers from across Europe fought alongside Habsburg troops in what was seen as a crusade, and the final assault was followed by a bloody sack. The city''s recapture was the main success of the Great Turkish War in Hungary and a symbol of the return of Hungary to Christian rule.'),
  ('Second Battle of Mohács', 1687, 'On 12 August 1687 an Imperial army under Charles of Lorraine decisively defeated the Ottomans at Harsány Mountain, near Mohács, the very place where Hungary had been crushed in 1526. The Ottomans lost most of their artillery, and the sultan, Mehmed IV, was overthrown later that year. The victory led to the reconquest of much of Hungary, and the Hungarian diet then accepted the Habsburgs as hereditary kings.'),
  ('Glorious Revolution', 1688, 'In November 1688 the Dutch stadtholder William of Orange landed in England with an army at the invitation of leading English nobles, and the Catholic King James II fled. William and his wife Mary, James''s Protestant daughter, became joint monarchs. The Bill of Rights of 1689 then limited the powers of the Crown and made Parliament supreme, which is why the Glorious Revolution is seen as a founding moment of English constitutional monarchy.'),
  ('Battle of Slankamen', 1691, 'On 19 August 1691 the Imperial army of Louis William of Baden defeated a larger Ottoman force at Slankamen, near the Danube in modern Serbia. The Grand Vizier, Mustafa Köprülü, an able reformer who had recovered Belgrade the year before, was killed by a stray bullet, and the Ottoman army fled leaving its war chest behind. The victory secured Habsburg control of Hungary and Transylvania and ended the Ottoman revival.'),
  ('Battle of Zenta', 1697, 'On 11 September 1697 Prince Eugene of Savoy caught the Ottoman army as it was crossing the Tisza River at Zenta and destroyed it, with thousands of Ottoman soldiers killed or drowned and the Grand Vizier among the dead. Sultan Mustafa II escaped but his army was shattered. The victory forced the Ottomans to sign the Treaty of Karlowitz in 1699, which gave Hungary and Transylvania to the Habsburgs, and made Eugene famous across Europe.'),
  ('Battle of Narva', 1700, 'On 30 November 1700 the 18-year-old Charles XII of Sweden led about 10,000 men through a snowstorm to attack a Russian siege army of 30,000 or more at Narva in Estonia, and routed it. Tsar Peter the Great had left the camp before the battle. The victory made Charles seem unbeatable, but he turned against Poland instead of finishing Russia, which gave Peter time to rebuild his army and eventually win the war.'),
  ('Battle of Blenheim', 1704, 'On 13 August 1704 the Duke of Marlborough and Prince Eugene of Savoy defeated a Franco-Bavarian army under Marshal Tallard at Blenheim on the Danube, and Tallard himself was captured. The Allied victory saved Vienna from the French and Bavarians and ended the belief that Louis XIV''s armies could not be beaten. It made Marlborough a national hero, and a grateful nation rewarded him with Blenheim Palace.'),
  ('Act of Union', 1707, 'On 1 May 1707 the Acts of Union merged the kingdoms of England and Scotland into the single Kingdom of Great Britain, with one parliament at Westminster. Scotland kept its own legal system and its Presbyterian church and gained free trade within the new state, while its parliament in Edinburgh was abolished. Many Scots accepted the union partly because the failed Darien colony had left the country in financial trouble.'),
  ('Battle of Poltava', 1709, 'On 8 July 1709 Tsar Peter the Great''s army defeated Charles XII''s Swedes at Poltava in Ukraine, in the largest battle of the Great Northern War. Charles, wounded in the foot beforehand, fled to the Ottoman Empire, and most of his army surrendered soon afterwards. The battle ended Sweden''s status as a great power and marked the rise of Russia as the leading power in eastern Europe.'),
  ('Battle of Pruth', 1711, 'In July 1711 Peter the Great marched into Moldavia and was surrounded by a much larger Ottoman army under Grand Vizier Baltacı Mehmed Pasha on the Pruth River. Ill-supplied and outnumbered, Peter negotiated a peace that gave up the port of Azov and his Black Sea ambitions, and promised to leave Polish affairs alone. It was a humiliating setback for the tsar, but he got his army out intact, and the Ottomans were criticized for letting him go.'),
  ('Battle of Petrovaradin', 1716, 'On 5 August 1716 Prince Eugene of Savoy attacked a much larger Ottoman army besieging the fortress of Petrovaradin, ''the Gibraltar of the Danube'', and routed it. The Grand Vizier, Damat Ali Pasha, was among the dead. The victory opened the way to the capture of Temesvár later that year and Belgrade in 1717, and it led to Habsburg gains at the Treaty of Passarowitz.'),
  ('Siege of Temesvár', 1716, 'From 31 August to 12 October 1716 Prince Eugene of Savoy besieged and took Temesvár, the last Ottoman stronghold in Hungary, which the Ottomans had held since 1552. The city was a key fortress controlling the Banat region. Its fall after 164 years ended Ottoman rule north of the Danube and Sava rivers, and the Habsburgs then organized the area as the Banat of Temeswar.'),
  ('Battle of Grocka', 1739, 'On 21-22 July 1739 the Ottoman army defeated the Habsburgs at Grocka, near Belgrade, in the decisive battle of the Austro-Turkish War of 1737-1739. The Ottomans then besieged Belgrade, and in the Treaty of Belgrade in September the Habsburgs gave back Belgrade and northern Serbia, which they had won in 1718. It was a rare Ottoman success in the eighteenth century and fixed the Sava and Danube rivers as the border for decades.');
